{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a sustained rise (new 30d highs / positive slope), the model is consistently too low. Apply an additive uplift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–3, larger by days 10–30).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors increase with lead time; near-term forecasts are only slightly low, but mid/long horizon (≈10–30d) are materially low. Use a ramping correction factor (increasing offset or multiplier) with forecast step.",
    "Trend/level lag: the forecast path follows the direction but lags the level shift (fails to rebase after a breakout). After a breakout above the recent range, shift the whole forecast trajectory upward rather than only steepening the slope.",
    "Over-imposed mean reversion: forecasts drift back toward ~70–73 even when realized prices stay elevated (~78–83). Reduce mean-reversion strength when recent volatility + momentum are high; anchor to a higher rolling mean (e.g., last 5–10d) in strong trends.",
    "Post-spike behavior: after sharp up moves, the model tends to fade/flatten too quickly versus outcomes that remain high for weeks. Replace quick fade with a 'sticky' regime: keep elevated levels for 1–3 weeks before allowing reversion."
  ]
}
